<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mongol invasions of India</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1297">1297</a>–<a href="/years/1306">1306</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mongol invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> were a series of attacks on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> of the Chagatai realm, peaking between 1297 and 1306. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> repeatedly repelled them, protecting northern India from the devastation suffered elsewhere. Mongol pressure in this era also shaped the wider Islamic world, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> held off Mongol armies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Mongol attacks on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> began in 1221, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> pursued the Khwarazmian prince Jalal al-Din to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> and sent commanders to chase him through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> region and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a>. Some time after 1235 a Mongol force made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> a dependency under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darughachi">darughachi</a>, and on 30 December 1241 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under Munggetu sacked Lahore. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a>'s general Sali Noyan attacked Multan and Lahore, but Hulegu refused to sanction a full invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. Sultan Ghiyas ud din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balban">Balban</a> (r. 1266-1287) made defence against the Mongols the chief concern of his reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>From the 1280s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> under Duwa Khan pressed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In the winter of 1297 the noyan Kadar ravaged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, and the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-khan">Ulugh Khan</a> defeated him at Jaran-Manjur on 6 February 1298. In late 1299 Duwa sent his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutlugh-khwaja">Qutlugh Khwaja</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>; at Kili the general Zafar Khan attacked without orders and was killed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> withdrew. Around August 1303 they ransacked Delhi while Alauddin held out in the unfinished Siri Fort. Malik <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> defeated them at the Battle of Amroha after December 1305, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a> routed another army near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> in 1306.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duwa">Duwa</a> died in 1307 and the raids ceased. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a>'s governor of Dipalpur, Malik Tughluq, then raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> each year. The siege of 1303 led Alauddin to strengthen the forts along the invasion routes and to carry out economic reforms to pay for a strong army. In 1327 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarmashirin">Tarmashirin</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, the last large invasion. Delhi's chroniclers described the conflicts between the Tengrist Mongols and the Muslim community in binary terms, though many of the sultanate's own elites were of Turkic or Mongol descent, and they gave the Mongol armies at 100,000 to 200,000 riders where they probably numbered 10,000 to 30,000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mongol invasions of India” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
